BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bridging Minds: Supporting Mental Health in the Early Years (0–5
 ) — Small Actions\, Big Impact
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261118T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261118T190000
UID:6ad92376035faf0033de27c8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Dr. Aliza Israel and Dr. Tatiana Valverde\, child psychiatrist
 s at Women’s College Hospital\, will explore why the early years of life
  are so important for children's mental health\, emotional well-being\, an
 d development. From birth to age five\, children begin to develop the foun
 dations for relationships\, emotional regulation\, learning\, and resilien
 ce. Through everyday interactions\, play\, and nurturing relationships\, p
 arents\, caregivers\, and communities can help create a strong foundation 
 for lifelong well-being.\nWhat to Expect:\nUnderstanding Early      Childh
 ood:\nWhy the first five years are an important period for brain      deve
 lopment\, emotional growth\, and lifelong mental health\nThe Power of     
  Relationships:\nHow responsive caregiving\, secure relationships\,      p
 lay\, and everyday interactions help young children feel safe\, connected\
 ,      and supported\nSupporting Big      Feelings:\nUnderstanding co-regu
 lation and how parents and caregivers can help      young children navigat
 e big emotions\, manage frustration\, and gradually      develop their own
  emotional regulation skills\nSmall Actions\,      Lasting Impact:\nPracti
 cal\, everyday ways families\, caregivers\,      and communities can suppo
 rt children's mental health and help give every      child a healthy start
 \nAbout the Presenters:\nDr. Aliza Israel is a child and adolescent psychi
 atrist at Women's College Hospital\, and lecturer at the University of Tor
 onto in child psychiatry and women's mental health. Her work focuses on th
 e interplay between maternal and child mental health\, and in promoting re
 silience in young families. Dr. Israel lives in Toronto\, Canada\, with he
 r husband and three rapidly-growing boys.  She is also an amateur juggler 
 who may or may not secretly wish to run away with the circus.\nDr. Tatiana
  Valverde da Conceicao is a Child and Adolescent Psychiatrist at the Repro
 ductive Life Stages Program at Women’s College Hospital. She’s an Assi
 stant Professor at University of Toronto. Her clinical work involves gener
 al Child and Adolescent Psychiatry with a special focus on Early Childhood
  Mental Health.  In addition to her clinical and academic work\, the exper
 ience of being a mother influences and enriches her professional perspecti
 ve.\nAbout the Series\nBridging Minds: Health & Science Conversations is a
  partnership between the Toronto Public Library and Women’s College Hosp
 ital. Through monthly discussions led by experts from across different cli
 nical areas and scientific disciplines\, this series provides attendees wi
 th the opportunity to engage directly with our topics and speakers and emp
 owers communities to navigate health and science topics confidently\, insp
 iring a future of informed decision-making.\nLearn more:\nhttps://www.wome
 nscollegehospital.ca/bridging-minds/\nReserve Your Spot Today!\nThis virtu
 al Zoom event is free to join but registration is required. Once registere
 d\, you’ll receive an email with the Zoom link.\nWe can’t wait to see 
 you there!
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad92376035faf0033de27c8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
